//===-- limits - `std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity()`, and nothing else -===//
//
// The `std::` sibling of `cuda/std/limits`, added for one site and shaped by
// exactly the same argument that file makes.
//
// # The site that asked
//
// FlashInfer's MoE glue kernels give three activation adaptors a default
// clamp of "no clamp":
//
// ```
//   csrc/fused_moe/cutlass_backend/cutlass_fused_moe_kernels.cuh:2012
//   struct GLUAdaptor { ... float limit = std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity(); };
//   :2026   struct SwigluBiasAdaptor  { ... same }
//   :2044   struct SwigluStepAdaptor  { ... float limit = 7.0f; }
// ```
//
// The third is the interesting one: `SwigluStepAdaptor` has a *finite*
// default, so the two spellings are load-bearing and the infinity is not
// decoration. `min(silu(gate), inf)` is `silu(gate)`; `min(silu(gate), 7)` is
// Step-3.5's clamp. A shim that got `infinity()` wrong would change the first
// two activations and leave the third correct, which is precisely the shape
// of bug this tree keeps recording.
//
// # Why it is a `__device__` function and not a `constexpr`
//
// Same as `cuda/std/limits`: `__int_as_float(0x7f800000u)` is the bit
// pattern, not a computation, and `0x7f800000` is `+INF` by definition of
// IEEE-754 binary32 rather than by any arithmetic that could round. A
// `constexpr float` initialised from `1.0f / 0.0f` is a division by zero in a
// constant expression; one initialised from `HUGE_VALF` needs `<cmath>`.
//
// The consequence is that `float limit = std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity();`
// as a **default member initialiser** is a runtime store, not a compile-time
// constant. That is fine here -- all three adaptors are constructed as
// `ActFn fn{};` inside a kernel body -- but it is why this file cannot simply
// hand back a `static constexpr float`.
//
// # What is deliberately absent
//
// `max()`, `lowest()`, `min()`, `denorm_min()`, `is_specialized`, every
// integer specialisation, and every other floating type. `cuda/std/limits`
// records the reasoning at length and it transfers unchanged: a limits header
// that grows a member per call site stops being a floor. The primary
// `numeric_limits` template below is **declared and not defined**, so
// `numeric_limits<double>` is an incomplete type at the point of use -- an
// error naming the type, not a silently different answer.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
#pragma once

namespace std {

template <class T>
struct numeric_limits;

template <>
struct numeric_limits<float> {
    static constexpr bool is_specialized = true;

    /// `+INF`, as the bit pattern it is.
    static __device__ __forceinline__ float infinity() { return __int_as_float(0x7f800000u); }
};

}  // namespace std
